---
import PageNav from "../components/page_nav.astro";
import PageFooter from "../components/page_footer.astro";

import "@fontsource/ibm-plex-sans/400.css";
import "@fontsource/ibm-plex-sans/600.css";
import "@fontsource/ibm-plex-sans/700.css";
import "@fontsource/ibm-plex-mono/400.css";
import "@fontsource/ibm-plex-mono/600.css";

const CONTACT = "info@openwanderer.com";

const LAST_UPDATED = "6 September 2026";
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>Privacy Policy | wanderer</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="How the wanderer project handles data across the website, the mobile apps, self-hosted instances and federation."
    />
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:title" content="Privacy Policy | wanderer" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" />
  </head>

  <body>
    <PageNav />

    <main class="legal">
      <header class="legal-head">
        <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          wanderer is self-hosted software. That changes who holds your data,
          so this policy starts by saying who is responsible for what. The
          rest follows from it.
        </p>
        <p class="updated">Last updated: {LAST_UPDATED}</p>
      </header>

      <section>
        <h2>1. Who is responsible for what</h2>
        <p>
          Three separate parties can be involved when you use wanderer, and
          they hold different data. Confusing them is the most common
          misunderstanding about this project, so they are named first.
        </p>
        <dl class="parties">
          <div>
            <dt>The wanderer project</dt>
            <dd>
              Publishes this website, the documentation, and the wanderer
              mobile apps in the App Store and Google Play. We operate no
              account system for the app and receive none of your trails,
              recordings, photos or account details.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Your instance operator</dt>
            <dd>
              The app connects to a wanderer instance that <em>you</em> choose:
              one you host yourself, one run by a community member, or a
              company's. That operator stores your account and your content,
              and is the data controller for it. Their privacy policy governs
              that data, not this one.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Other instances, through federation</dt>
            <dd>
              If your instance is connected to others, content you have marked
              public can be copied to those instances and stored by their
              operators. See <a href="#federation">section 8</a>.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
        <p>
          Everything below distinguishes between data that stays on your
          device, data that goes to your chosen instance, and the small number
          of cases where something reaches a third party.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>2. This website</h2>
        <p>
          openwanderer.com serves documentation and information about the
          project. It has no user accounts, no login, and no comment system.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>No analytics or tracking.</strong> The site runs no analytics,
            no tracking pixels and no advertising, and sets no tracking cookies.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No third-party fonts or embeds.</strong> Fonts are served from
            this site, not from a font CDN, so visiting a page does not disclose
            your IP address to a third party.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Server logs.</strong> Our hosting provider records standard
            request data (IP address, timestamp, requested URL, referrer and
            user agent) to deliver the site and defend against abuse. These
            logs are not combined with anything else and are not used to profile
            visitors.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Links leave us.</strong> Pages link to GitHub, Discord and
            other sites. Once you follow such a link, that site's own policy
            applies.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>3. The app: data on your device</h2>
        <p>
          Most of what the app holds never leaves your phone unless you send it.
          Stored locally are:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Trails you have saved or downloaded, including their route geometry,
            waypoints and photos.
          </li>
          <li>
            Map regions you download for offline use, and the map tiles inside
            them.
          </li>
          <li>
            Recordings in progress, including the track recorded so far, so a
            recording survives the app being closed.
          </li>
          <li>
            Your session with your chosen instance, and app settings such as
            theme and units.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Uninstalling the app deletes all of it. Clearing the app's storage in
          Android or iOS settings has the same effect.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>4. The app: data sent to your instance</h2>
        <p>
          The app is a client for the instance you sign in to. What it sends
          there is what you ask it to send: your account details and profile,
          trails you create or save, recorded tracks you choose to save, photos
          you attach, comments, lists and summit logs.
        </p>
        <p>
          That instance stores this data under its own operator's control and
          its own policy. If you host your instance yourself, that operator is
          you. We have no access to it, cannot retrieve it, and cannot delete it
          on your behalf.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="location">
        <h2>5. The app: location data</h2>
        <p>
          Location is the most sensitive thing the app handles, so it is set out
          in full.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>When it is collected.</strong> Only while you are actively
            recording a trail or navigating one. Both start on an explicit
            action by you, and both run with a persistent notification that
            stays visible for as long as they are running. The app does not
            collect location at other times.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Background collection.</strong> Recording and navigation
            continue when the screen is off, when another app is in front, and
            when the app has been cleared from recent apps. This is why the app
            asks for background location access. Without it, recording and
            navigation stop as soon as the app is closed and the rest of the
            trail is lost. Before that permission is requested, the app shows an
            in-app explanation of what it is for.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Where it goes.</strong> The recorded track is written to your
            device as you walk. It is transmitted to your instance only when you
            choose to save the trail. If you discard a recording, it never
            leaves the device.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>What it is used for.</strong> Drawing your position on the
            map, turn-by-turn guidance, and building the saved trail: its
            track, distance, elevation gain and duration. Location is not used
            for advertising, profiling or analytics, and is not sold or shared
            with data brokers.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>6. The app: permissions</h2>
        <dl class="parties">
          <div>
            <dt>Location (while using the app)</dt>
            <dd>
              Shows where you are on the map, and records or navigates a trail.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Location (all the time / background)</dt>
            <dd>
              Keeps a recording or navigation session running when the app is
              not in front. Optional: declining leaves the app working, but a
              session ends when you close the app.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Notifications</dt>
            <dd>
              Shows the persistent notification for a running recording or
              navigation, and progress while a map region downloads. Without it
              a session can run without being visible to you, which is why the
              app asks.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Photos and files</dt>
            <dd>
              Only when you attach a photo to a trail or import a GPX, KML or
              similar file. The app reads what you pick and nothing else.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>7. The app: third parties</h2>
        <p>
          Besides the instance you choose, the app contacts very little. In
          full:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Avatar images (api.dicebear.com).</strong> When an account has
            no profile picture, the app requests a generated placeholder from
            DiceBear, sending the display name or username so the initials
            match. No other account data is sent. Requests carry your IP address
            to that service, as any web request does.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Map data and routing.</strong> Map tiles, search and route
            calculation are served by your instance, using whichever providers
            its operator has configured. Your instance operator's policy covers
            that.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>External map apps.</strong> If you tap to open a location in
            another app such as Google Maps, Apple Maps or Organic Maps,
            the coordinates are handed to that app. This only happens when you
            tap it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>App stores.</strong> Downloads and updates go through Apple
            and Google, who record their own data about installs. We receive
            only aggregate statistics, never anything identifying you.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="callout">
          The app contains no analytics SDK, no crash-reporting SDK, no
          advertising SDK and no social-media SDK. There is nothing in it that
          reports your behaviour back to us, because there is nowhere for it to
          report to.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="federation">
        <h2>8. Federation: how public content travels</h2>
        <p>
          wanderer instances can connect to one another using ActivityPub, the
          same protocol Mastodon uses. If your instance operator has connected
          it to others, this affects where your content ends up, so it is worth
          understanding before you post publicly.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Trails, lists and summit logs federate only when public.</strong>
            Marking one of these public is what makes it eligible to be copied to
            connected instances. Content that is private or shared with specific
            people is never sent this way.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Comments are the exception.</strong> A comment is delivered to
            the people it concerns regardless of whether the trail it is on is
            public or private: if you <code>@mention</code> a remote account, the
            comment text is sent to that account's server, and a copy also goes to
            the trail's author if their account is on another instance. The
            receiving server can reject it, but by then the text has already left
            your instance. In practice, this means a comment on a private trail can
            still leave your instance if it mentions someone, or if the trail's
            author is on a different one. Nothing about the trail itself travels
            with the comment, neither its title nor its description nor its
            location, only a reference to which trail it replies to.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Copies live on other servers.</strong> A federated copy is
            stored by the operator of the receiving instance, under their
            control and their policy. You remain the author, and editing rights
            stay with your own instance.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Unpublishing is not deletion.</strong> Making a public trail
            or list private again stops it from being sent onward, but no
            deletion is sent, so copies already held by other instances stay
            where they are. For trails specifically, this has a sharper
            consequence: if
            you make a trail private and delete it afterwards, no deletion signal
            is sent either, because deletion itself is skipped for content that is
            not currently public. If you want a deletion to reach instances that
            already received the trail, delete it while it is still public rather
            than unpublishing first.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Deletion is a request, not a guarantee.</strong> Deleting
            public content sends a delete out to instances that received it.
            Well-behaved instances honour it. An instance that is offline,
            misconfigured or hostile may not. Treat anything published publicly
            to a federated network as difficult to fully retract.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Departing accounts travel too.</strong> When an account is
            deleted, its instance announces that to the instances following it,
            and this one acts on such a notice by removing its copy of that
            account along with the content of theirs it was holding. Your own
            account leaving is announced the same way. See
            <a href="#retention">section 12</a> for what that does and does not
            cover. A notice is only ever accepted for the account that signed
            it: another server cannot ask this one to delete somebody else.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Who you federate with is your operator's choice.</strong> If
            you run your own instance, it is yours.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>9. Security and safeguards</h2>
        <p>
          This section states plainly what protects your data in transit and in
          storage, and what does not. We would rather say this clearly than let
          you assume more than the software provides.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>In transit.</strong> wanderer does not ship or run its own TLS
            termination. Whether your instance is served over HTTPS is entirely
            your instance operator's responsibility, and the app connects using
            whatever scheme your instance's address uses. On Android, the app
            itself blocks plaintext (cleartext) network traffic to everything
            except a local loopback connection used for offline map tiles, so it
            cannot be tricked into talking to a remote server over plain HTTP.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Between instances.</strong> Activities exchanged between
            federated instances are signed with HTTP Signatures, so a receiving
            instance can verify which server actually sent them. This
            authenticates the sender. It does not encrypt the content: a signed
            activity is still readable plaintext in transit, protected only by
            whatever transport encryption (see above) is in place.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Passwords.</strong> Stored hashed by your instance's
            authentication system, never in readable form.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Files.</strong> Photos, GPX files and avatars are served from
            a direct URL. There is no additional access token guarding that URL.
            An unguessable filename is the only thing standing between a link and
            the file it points to, and anyone who has or guesses that link can
            retrieve the file.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>At rest.</strong> Content is not encrypted at rest by
            wanderer. Whatever protection your data has on disk, whether that is
            full-disk encryption, a secured server, or none at all, is your
            instance operator's decision, not something this project provides.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>On the device.</strong> As described in section 3, the local
            database, downloaded map regions and your session are held inside the
            app's own OS-managed sandbox and removed when you uninstall it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>In-app disclosures.</strong> Before background location is
            requested the app shows an explanation of what it is for, and a
            persistent notification stays visible for as long as recording or
            navigation is running. See <a href="#location">section 5</a>. The app
            contains no advertising or analytics SDKs, and location is never sold
            or shared with data brokers.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>10. Demo instance</h2>
        <p>
          A public demo instance is linked from this site so people can try
          wanderer without installing it. It is for evaluation only: accounts
          and content on it may be reset or deleted at any time, without notice
          and without backup. Do not put anything there you would mind losing or
          anything you would mind others seeing.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>11. Legal bases</h2>
        <p>
          Where the GDPR applies, we rely on the following legal bases for the
          limited processing we carry out:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Art. 6(1)(f), legitimate interests</strong> for serving this
            website securely and keeping server logs to defend against abuse.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Art. 6(1)(a), consent</strong> for device permissions such as
            location, background location and notifications, each of which you
            grant and can withdraw in your device settings at any time.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Processing carried out by your instance operator has its own legal
          basis, which they are responsible for stating.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="retention">
        <h2>12. Retention and deletion</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>On your device:</strong> kept until you delete the content or
            uninstall the app.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>On your instance:</strong> governed by your instance
            operator's retention policy. Deleting your account removes your
            login credentials, your public profile, and the content you
            authored: your trails, lists, summit logs and the comments you left
            on other people's trails. Comments you wrote go with the account
            even where they sit under someone else's trail, so a thread you took
            part in may read differently afterwards.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Activity records are not covered by account or content
            deletion.</strong> Your instance keeps a record of the federation
            activities it sent on your behalf, meaning the ActivityPub messages
            announcing your public trails, lists and summit logs to connected
            instances. These records embed a snapshot of the content as it was
            when it was sent, including its description and location
            coordinates where relevant, and neither deleting that content nor
            deleting your account clears them. This record is also readable by
            anyone who has or guesses its address, without needing to log in.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Federated copies:</strong> once a piece of public content has
            reached another instance, whether as a live copy or as one of the
            activity snapshots described above, removing it there depends on that
            instance honouring a delete signal from yours. See
            <a href="#federation">section 8</a>. Deleting a single trail, list or
            summit log while your account still exists sends that signal for
            that item. Deleting your whole account sends one signal for the
            account itself rather than one per item, which asks receiving
            instances to drop everything of yours; the individual items cannot
            each announce their own removal, because your profile goes before
            they do and there is no longer an identity to sign in your name. In
            both cases the content is removed from your own instance regardless.
            The account notice reaches the instances that follow you, which are
            the only ones a departing account can still be addressed to, so an
            instance that holds a copy without following you may never be told.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Website server logs:</strong> kept only as long as needed for
            security and troubleshooting, then deleted.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>13. Your rights</h2>
        <p>
          Under the GDPR you have the right to access, rectify, erase, restrict
          and port your data, to object to processing, and to complain to a
          supervisory authority.
        </p>
        <p>
          Direct these to whoever holds the data. For your account, trails,
          photos and recordings, that is <strong>your instance operator</strong>.
          We cannot act on data we do not hold. For this website, use the
          contact details below.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>14. Children</h2>
        <p>
          The app and this site are not directed at children and we do not
          knowingly collect data from them. Age requirements for an account are
          set by the instance you sign up to.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>15. Changes</h2>
        <p>
          This policy may change as the software does. The date at the top
          reflects the current version, and material changes will be noted in
          the project changelog.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>16. Contact</h2>
        <p>
          For questions about this policy or about data held by the project:
        </p>
        <address>
          <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>
        </address>
        <p class="muted">
          For data held on a wanderer instance you use, contact that instance's
          operator instead. We have no access to it.
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>

    <PageFooter />
  </body>
</html>

<style>
  /* ── Tokens ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /* Standalone pages are outside Starlight, so its `customCss` never loads
     here and none of the tailwind.css variables exist. Declare the same design
     tokens the other pages under src/pages do. page_footer.astro reads
     --polar-night directly, and without it the footer renders transparent with
     invisible white links. */
  :root {
    --polar-night: #242734;
    --deep-void: #12141c;
    --surface-dark: #1e2028;
    --carbon: #262831;
    --dusk-divide: #374151;
    --summit-fog: #94a3b8;
    --white: #ffffff;
  }

  body {
    margin: 0;
    background: var(--deep-void);
    color: var(--white);
    font-family: "IBM Plex Sans", -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, sans-serif;
    -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased;
  }

  .legal {
    max-width: 46rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: clamp(3rem, 8vw, 6rem) clamp(1.25rem, 5vw, 2.5rem) 6rem;
  }

  .legal-head {
    padding-bottom: 2.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--dusk-divide);
  }

  .eyebrow {
    font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", monospace;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--summit-fog);
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.25rem, 5vw, 3rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    line-height: 1.1;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.75);
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
  }

  .updated {
    font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", monospace;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--summit-fog);
    margin: 0;
  }

  section {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: 1.375rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    line-height: 1.25;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  p {
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8);
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  ul {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }

  li {
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8);
  }

  strong {
    color: var(--white);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .parties {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.25rem;
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .parties > div {
    border-left: 2px solid var(--dusk-divide);
    padding-left: 1.125rem;
  }

  dt {
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 1rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.375rem;
    color: var(--white);
  }

  dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8);
  }

  .callout {
    border: 1px solid var(--dusk-divide);
    border-radius: 8px;
    background: var(--surface-dark);
    padding: 1rem 1.125rem;
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.9);
  }

  address {
    font-style: normal;
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8);
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .muted {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--summit-fog);
  }

  a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
  }
  a:hover {
    color: var(--white);
  }
</style>
